<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <!--
      docs/plan/03 § src — the identity module (docs/plan/11), ADR-015.

      The grains, the credential implementations, the sign-in path with its enumeration and timing
      hardening, and the refresh chain with reuse detection. The OAuth/OIDC protocol itself is not
      here: that is OpenIddict, in CyberCloud.Identity.Host, which is what ADR-015 means by "it
      handles the protocol, we own the stores, and the stores are grains".

      ⚠ NO ASP.NET, AND THAT IS WHAT KEEPS THE TWO-HOST SPLIT HONEST.
      docs/plan/11 § Hosts makes cookies-here-bearer-there a security boundary. This assembly has no
      opinion about either, because it cannot name a cookie or a bearer scheme — the authentication
      surface lives entirely in the host. An assembly that could reach HttpContext would eventually
      grow a helper that reads one, and the boundary would become a convention again.

      ⚠ IT REFERENCES CyberCloud.Authorization, NOT ONLY ITS .Contracts, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
      docs/plan/11 § The object model puts group membership in ReBAC tuples rather than in grain
      state, so IGroupGrain writes `group:X#member@user:Y`. Spelling `group`, `member` and `user` as
      string literals here would be a second copy of the schema's vocabulary, and the failure mode of
      a second copy is a membership write that no membership check can see. ObjectTypes and Relations
      live in the implementation assembly, so that is what is referenced. This does NOT make identity
      an authorization enforcement point — docs/plan/07 § The enforcement seam keeps that in the
      resource manager; what happens here is tuple WRITES, which is the same thing a role assignment
      is.

      ⚠ IT REFERENCES CyberCloud.Communication.Contracts, AND THE DIRECTION IS THE POINT.
      docs/plan/11 § Credentials routes email, SMS and WhatsApp one-time codes through
      CyberCloud.Communication (docs/plan/17), and IOtpDeliverySeam is where that lands. The adapter —
      Seams/CommunicationOtpDelivery.cs — lives HERE and not there, so that CyberCloud.Communication
      keeps referencing nothing in identity, monitor or billing: a sending module that knew about
      identity would make a carrier outage an identity outage, and would put a second module in the
      dependency path of every sign-in.

      ⚠ NO ARCHITECTURE GATE ENFORCED THAT DIRECTION, and pretending otherwise would have been worse
      than the gap. docs/plan/03 § Assembly graph rules had six rules; rule 2 ("no Providers.*
      assembly references another") was the only one about sibling modules and it is scoped to
      CyberCloud.Providers.* by name — Build.Architecture.cs's ProviderFamily() returns null for
      anything else. So a CyberCloud.Communication -> CyberCloud.Identity.Contracts reference would
      compile and pass ./build.sh Architecture.

      ⚠ CORRECTED 2026-08-12: it does now, and the paragraph above is kept because it is what the
      hole looked like from here. Rule 7 (module layering) was added after exactly that reference was
      constructed — a real ProjectReference plus a bound type, so the AssemblyRef table had a row —
      and the gate stayed green over it. module-layering.txt at the repository root is the reviewed
      declaration; the reverse edge is not in it, and could not be added, because
      CyberCloud.Identity -> CyberCloud.Communication is, and the declaration must be acyclic.

      It is .Contracts and never the implementation: IMessageSender and SendRequest are all this
      needs, and referencing the grains would put the sending module's activation graph in identity's
      silo whether or not the host asked for it.

      ⚠ IT REFERENCES CyberCloud.Tenancy.Contracts FOR IEmailIndexGrain AND NOTHING ELSE.
      docs/plan/06 § Grain keys already owns per-tenant email uniqueness and docs/plan/11 § Sign-up
      names that grain by name. Re-implementing the claim here would be a second uniqueness mechanism
      over the same digest, which is the one place a duplicate is a correctness bug.
    -->

    <ItemGroup>
        <!-- Grain implementations and [PersistentState]. -->
        <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Orleans.Server"/>
        <!-- IGrainFactory.ForTenant — every grain reference in this assembly goes through it. -->
        <PackageReference Include="Orleans.Multitenant"/>
        <!--
          ⚠ NOT IN docs/plan/02's DEPENDENCY REGISTER. See the block in Directory.Packages.props for
          what was surveyed and why this one won; the short version is that .NET ships no Argon2,
          NSec cannot express p=4, and docs/plan/11 § Credentials specifies p=4. That document's own
          rule makes this an ADR request rather than a build fix.
        -->
        <PackageReference Include="Konscious.Security.Cryptography.Argon2"/>
        <!--
          The lockout counter is a Redis INCR, not a grain — docs/plan/11 § Credentials. This is the
          client for it. ⚠ It is the ONLY direct hot-tier access in the module: everything else goes
          through Orleans grain storage, and the reason this one does not is the whole point of the
          row — a grain call on the failure path of an unauthenticated endpoint is the amplifier that
          paragraph exists to forbid.
        -->
        <PackageReference Include="StackExchange.Redis"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Core/CyberCloud.Core.csproj"/>
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Core.Contracts/CyberCloud.Core.Contracts.csproj"/>
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Identity.Contracts/CyberCloud.Identity.Contracts.csproj"/>
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Communication.Contracts/CyberCloud.Communication.Contracts.csproj"/>
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Tenancy.Contracts/CyberCloud.Tenancy.Contracts.csproj"/>
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Authorization.Contracts/CyberCloud.Authorization.Contracts.csproj"/>
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Authorization/CyberCloud.Authorization.csproj"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup Label="CyberCloud.Analyzers — see CyberCloud.Core.csproj for why this is not a reference">
        <!--
          CC1005 is the rule that earns its keep here: this is the module most likely to trip it, and
          tripping it is the point. Credentials are SecretRef handles and one-way hashes; the two
          members that end in a banned suffix and are genuinely not secrets carry a per-member
          [SuppressMessage] with the argument, never a NoWarn on the assembly.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Analyzers/CyberCloud.Analyzers.csproj"
                          OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                          ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"/>
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
